<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wind of Change speech</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1960">1960</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Wind of Change</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Wind of Change speech was an address delivered by the British prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-macmillan">Harold Macmillan</a> to the parliament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a> in 1960. He acknowledged the strength of African national consciousness and signalled that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> would not resist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decolonisation">decolonisation</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> treat it as a marker of the rapid dismantling of the empire in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p></div>
